Q. how to implement accessibility?
A. Accessibility (a11y) means making a web application usable by everyone, 
	including people with visual, hearing, motor, cognitive or other disabilities.
	For both Angular and React, the primary target should be WCAG 2.2 Level AA (Web Content Accessibility Guidelines).

	Accessibility fundamentals : 
	1. Semantic HTML : Always use HTML elements according to their intended purpose
		if have to show button then use button tag, do not use div and with style show it as button.
		use : <header> for page headers.
				<nav> for navigation.
				<main> for main content.
				<section> for logical sections.
				<button> for actions.
				<a> for navigation.
				<form> for forms.
				<label> for form labels.
	2. Images and alternative text : Every meaningful image should have descriptive alternative text.
		example : <img src="profile.jpg" alt="Customer profile photograph">
		for decoretive or style images , left it as blank
		example : <img src="background.png" alt="">
	3. Keyboard accessibility : Users must be able to operate the application without a mouse.
		Key				Purpose
		Tab				Move to next focusable element
		Shift + Tab		Move to previous element
		Enter			Activate links and buttons
		Space			Activate buttons and checkboxes
		Escape			Close dialogs and menus
		Arrow keys		Navigate certain widgets
	4. ARIA attributes : ARIA (Accessible Rich Internet Applications) provides additional accessibility information to assistive technologies.
		example : <button aria-label="Close dialog"> X </button>
	5. Color contrast : Text should have sufficient contrast against its background.
		WCAG AA requirements:
		- Normal text: minimum 4.5:1.
		- Large text: minimum 3:1.
		- UI components and meaningful graphical objects: minimum 3:1 against adjacent colors.
		Do not communicate errors or status using color alone.
	6. Accessible forms : Every input should have a properly associated label.
		Use appropriate error messages and associate them with their respective fields.
		<input type="email" id="email" name="email" required aria-describedby="email-error" />
		<span id="email-error"> Please enter a valid email address. </span>
	7. Accessible tables : Use semantic table elements.
		example : inside table if have to show heading : <caption>Customer information</caption>
	8. Accessible dialogs and modals
		A modal should:
		- Receive focus when opened.
		- Keep keyboard focus inside while open.
		- Close using Escape when appropriate.
		- Return focus to the triggering element when closed.
		- Have an accessible name.
		- Prevent interaction with background content while modal.
	9. Responsive accessibility
		Ensure:
		- Text can be zoomed to 200% without loss of content.
		- Layout works at narrow viewport widths.
		- Content does not require horizontal scrolling unnecessarily.
		- Touch targets meet WCAG 2.2 AA requirements, generally at least 24 × 24 CSS pixels, subject to exceptions.
	10. Dynamic content announcements
		When API responses, errors or status messages update dynamically, make them available to screen readers.
		<div aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
		  Data loaded successfully.
		</div>

	Part 2: Complete accessibility implementation in Angular 20
	1. Install accessibility package : Angular provides the CDK accessibility module and Angular Aria directives.
			npm install @angular/cdk
			npm install @angular/aria
			
			The packages provide:
			- Focus management.
			- Focus trapping.
			- Screen reader announcements.
			- Keyboard navigation support.
			- Accessible UI interaction patterns.

	2. Create an accessible application layout : Create a application layout with semantic HTML in including form and all sections .
	3. If have to read api response or responseMessage : Install/use Angular CDK LiveAnnouncer
			notification.service.ts
		import { Injectable, inject } from '@angular/core';
		import { LiveAnnouncer } from '@angular/cdk/a11y';
		
		@Injectable({
		  providedIn: 'root'
		})
		export class NotificationService {
		
		  private announcer = inject(LiveAnnouncer);
		
		  announce(message: string) {
		    this.announcer.announce(message, 'polite');
		  }
		}

		Use it inside a component:
		this.notificationService.announce(
		  'Customer data loaded successfully'
		);
	4. For modal dialogues : Install CDK if not already installed.
		Use cdkTrapFocus to keep keyboard focus inside the dialog.
		also implement:
		- Initial focus on an appropriate control.
		- Escape-key handling.
		- Focus restoration to the opener.
		- Background interaction prevention.
		- Proper dialog lifecycle.

		import { Component } from '@angular/core';
		import { A11yModule } from '@angular/cdk/a11y';
		
		@Component({
		  selector: 'app-confirm-dialog',
		  standalone: true,
		  imports: [A11yModule],
		  template: `
		    <section
		      role="dialog"
		      aria-modal="true"
		      aria-labelledby="dialog-title"
		      cdkTrapFocus
		      cdkTrapFocusAutoCapture
		    >
		      <h2 id="dialog-title">Confirm Action</h2>
		
		      <p>Do you want to continue?</p>
		
		      <button type="button" (click)="close()">
		        Cancel
		      </button>
		
		      <button type="button" (click)="confirm()">
		        Confirm
		      </button>
		    </section>
		  `
		})
		export class ConfirmDialogComponent {
		
		  close() {}
		
		  confirm() {}
		}

	5. Implement accessible routing : When navigating between Angular routes, move focus to the new page's main heading.

